Sometimes a syntax error can ripple into a myriad of errors, so if this
is the case(might not be) you will want to see what the first error reported
is. On unix/linux you can redirect the output to a file and open the file
with a text editor to see the first error.  I don't know which shell
cygwin uses, but with bash I would do something like this:

make 2> make.log

the above will include just the errors, to get everything in the log
file do something like this:

rm make.log
make >> make.log 2>> make.log

You won't need to do the rm line the first time you compile, but after
that you can do it to give you a clean slate because the '>>' appends
rather than overwrites the file like a single '>' would do.  The >>
will include the standard output and the 2>> will redirect the standard
error output.  Not sure if any of this will be of help in your build
environment, so if it doesn't, sorry about that.

